    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\r\n\r\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\r\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\r\n\r\n### Module(s)\r\n\r\nmini.ai\r\n\r\n### Description\r\n\r\nHi, following on from https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/issues/261 I have been attempting to create text objects for `w`, `W` and `p`. I have made some headway with `w`, it currently looks like this:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\n          w = { '[^%w@_]*()()[%w@_]+()%s*()[^%w@_]*' },\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe problem is when I input `2yiw` for example, it seems to want to yank only the second \"word\". Default vim behaviour would be to select both the current \"word\" and the next one. I'm guessing this is to do with the way `v:count` is implemented for text objects?\r\n\r\nI don't know whether this is a bug or a feature, for example I know with bracket-type text objects, the behaviour of v:count with a count of 2 would be to select the brackets surrounding the ones you're in. (i.e. to expand outwards). But I think for things like `word`s and `WORD`s it makes sense to expand forwards.\r\n\r\nI'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with `v:count`?",
    "closed_at": "2023-03-12T16:26:46Z",
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I'm wondering if it makes sense for text objects to be able to specify how they interact with `v:count`?\r\n\r\nTextobject interacts with `v:count` as if user consecutively selected it `v:count1` times. So `v3a)` is identical to `va)a)a)`.\r\n\r\nHow textobject behaves in consecutive selection depends on `search_method` and textobject specification itself. If `search_method` contains \"cover\" (like `cover`, `cover_or_next`, etc.), it will attempt to find smallest region which covers current selection (start is before, end is after) and *matches specification*.\r\n\r\nIn your case this pattern doesn't seem to match two words separated by whitespace (like `abc def`), so it can not find covering match. If you use default search method (`cover_or_next`), it will attempt to find *next* match (start is after, end is after) which is what happens from your description.\r\n\r\nI am honestly not sure how to make it better using Lua patterns. One other approach might be to use function which returns array of regions, but it might be computationally expensive and not trivial. ",
